[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Exception Testing with JUnit 5: assertThrows and assertDoesNotThrow"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Robust software development requires not only verifying that code produces correct results under normal conditions, but also ensuring it handles erroneous conditions gracefully. JUnit 5 provides powerful assertions like assertThrows and assertDoesNotThrow to handle exception testing effectively."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Testing Expected Exceptions with assertThrows"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The assertThrows method is used to verify that a specific block of code throws a designated exception. If the expected exception is thrown, the test passes. If no exception, or a different exception, is thrown, the test fails."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"@Test
void testDivisionByZero() {
    Calculator calc = new Calculator();
    assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class, () -> {
        calc.divide(10, 0);
    });
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also capture the thrown exception instance to assert additional properties, such as the exception message or nested causes."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"@Test
void testExceptionMessage() {
    Calculator calc = new Calculator();
    IllegalArgumentException exception = assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class, () -> {
        calc.divide(10, 0);
    });
    assertEquals("Division by zero is not allowed", exception.getMessage());
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Ensuring No Exceptions Are Thrown with assertDoesNotThrow"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Conversely, assertDoesNotThrow validates that a piece of execution completes without throwing any unexpected exceptions. While normal test execution automatically fails if an uncaught exception is thrown, using this assertion explicitly documents your intent."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"@Test
void testValidDivision() {
    Calculator calc = new Calculator();
    int result = assertDoesNotThrow(() -> {
        return calc.divide(10, 2);
    });
    assertEquals(5, result);
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Exception Testing"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Be specific: Always assert the most precise exception class possible (e.g., NullPointerException instead of just RuntimeException or Exception)."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep lambdas short: Limit the code inside the lambda passed to assertThrows to only the statement expected to trigger the exception."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Validate messages: When applicable, assert exception messages to ensure the correct error path was triggered for the user or logs."}]
